u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 4. SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\nBayesian update (Posterior = Prior \u00d7 Likelihood / Normalization):\n- Prior P(margin \u22655.5% by FY30) = 30% [Assumption, base rate].\n- Evidence: Q2 FY26 gross margin +50bp, revenue margin +60bp [Estimate, research brief]; membership = 35.8% of op income [Actual]; but Q3 FY26 adjusted EPS missed consensus ($1.28 vs $1.32) [Estimate] and COP at 7-year high [Estimate]. Likelihood ratio \u2248 1.3 \u2192 Posterior \u2248 40%.\n\n| Scenario | Prob | Assumptions | IV/share |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Bear | 25% | Rev +5-6% CAGR; COP depreciation (BBVA path) \u22123%/yr; op margin 3.8% by FY30; Chile capital sink; FY30 FCF $95M | $62 |\n| Base | 50% | Rev +10%/-1.5% FX/tapering; op margin 4.4\u21925.2%; capex normalizes 2031+; FCF terminal $267M | $102 |\n| Bull | 25% | Rev +11% sustained; op margin 5.6% by FY30 (membership fee hikes, platinum mix); FY30 FCF $244M; terminal FCF $300M | $140 |\n\n- Expected Price (EV) = 0.25\u00d762 + 0.50\u00d7102 + 0.25\u00d7140 = $101.5 \u2248 $102 [Arithmetic]\n- 50% CI: $85-130; 80% CI: $65-140 [Estimate]\n- Margin of Safety at T0 $174.29: (102 \u2212 174.29)/102 = \u221270.9% \u2192 NONE. Expected price is 42% BELOW current price.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 4.5. TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\nMarket-implied horizon: at 8.7% discount, $174.29 embeds ~10+ years of my base-case earnings, i.e., the market is capitalizing the Chile/membership story far beyond any guided period. My horizon: 3-5 years. Spread = +5 years \u2014 the wrong direction for a long. No long-side arbitrage; the spread is evidence of over-extension. No guidance exists beyond FY2026 in the data pack [Unconfirmed]; the Q3 FY26 transcript excerpt contains no forward-year figures [Actual, Section 10].\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 5. CONVICTION & POSITIONING\n\nStep 5-1. Conviction Score (0-3 each):\n1. Business quality: 3/3 | 2. Valuation attractiveness: 0/3 | 3. Macro alignment: 1/3 | 4. Thesis clarity: 3/3 | 5. Execution traceability: 3/3 \u2192 TOTAL 10/15 = HIGH (in the SELL thesis).\nConviction decay: -1 every 6 months without confirming evidence.\n\nStep 5-3. Sizing: For the long side, ZERO \u2014 no margin of safety. If already held, SELL/REDUCE. Do not initiate a short: high-quality compounder + 9.76% short float makes squeezes dangerous [Aggregator].\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 5.5. BEHAVIORAL & POSITIONING\n\n- Short interest: 9.76% of float, 6.15 days to cover, MoM +3.0 [Aggregator] \u2014 elevated; contrarian long signal; but justified by valuation.\n- Institutional ownership: 98.94% [Aggregator] \u2014 stock is institutionally saturated; limited marginal-buyer pool; ETF-flow sensitive.\n- Insider: 7.42% ownership [Aggregator]; no cluster-buy signal in pack.\n- Analyst targets: mean $153.33 vs price $174.29 \u2014 stock is 14% above the HIGH target [Aggregator]. Extreme positive sentiment vs. sell-side.\n- Price: 13% below 52-wk high $199.84 [Aggregator] \u2014 momentum still positive but breached analyst views.\n- Crowding Risk Score: 12/15 (institutional saturation + price > all targets + low float + high short) \u2192 HIGH.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 6. PORTFOLIO FIT\n\nPSMT is a defensive LatAm consumer franchise with 0.79% dividend yield [Aggregator] and beta 0.79 [Aggregator]. At 34.6x trailing earnings [Aggregator] it no longer functions as a defensive value anchor; it is a growth-price asset with EM-FX earnings risk. In a diversified book, holding it here sacrifices ~70% expected downside protection for a defensive label. Do NOT hold for yield (0.79% vs. 10Y 4.71%).\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 6.5. KELLY SIZING\n\nLong: Edge = (102 \u2212 174.29)/174.29 = \u221241.5% \u2192 Kelly = 0%.\nShort: Edge = 41.5%; probability of price < $174 in 3 yrs \u2248 75% [Estimate]; adverse odds if price goes to $220 \u2248 26%: Kelly \u2248 p \u2212 (1\u2212p)/b = 0.75 \u2212 0.25/1.6 \u2248 0.59 \u2014 absurdly high; capped by crowding and quality. Practical short: 0% \u2014 do not short; AVOID.\nLayer 5/Layer 6.5 gap: both are 0% for longs \u2014 no conflict.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 7. RISKS & CATALYSTS\n\nBull catalysts: Chile first-club opening (spring 2027) exceeds sales expectations; membership fee increase; COP depreciation reverses; margin accelerates via platinum mix; Costco/Sam's stay out of Central America.\nBear risks: COP/BRL? (COP alone) mean-reversion to BBVA's 3,600+ (15% translation drag) [Estimate]; Chile cost overruns; EM consumer slowdown; Q3 FY26 EPS miss reveals cost creep; executive transition risk [Estimate, research brief].\nKey events: Q4 FY26 earnings (Oct 2026); Montego Bay club opening (fall 2026); Chile Las Condes opening (spring 2027).\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 7.5. CORRELATION-ADJUSTED RISK\n\nCommon bear triggers with a LatAm/EM portfolio: USD strength (COP path via BBVA), EM election risk, commodity shocks, global recession compressing consumer spending. Tail correlation: PSMT's 0.79 beta understates correlation to LatAm FX \u2014 in a 2022-style dollar rally, PSMT's USD-reported earnings fall while its \"defensive\" label fails. CVaR estimate: expected loss in the 5% worst case \u2248 \u221245% over 12 months [Assumption]. Portfolio recommendation: replace with USD-functional staples if exposure to EM staples is desired.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 8. MONITORING RULES\n\nIf held, sell in tranches on any bounce toward $175-199; set hard stop-thought at $165 (analyst high). Re-entry if price \u2264 $110 (\u224835% below T0) AND: (1) constant-currency comps \u2265 +6% for 2 quarters; (2) FY27 EPS tracking \u2265 $6.50; (3) COP does not depreciate >10% from 3,100.\nKPI thresholds (Thesis KPIs from Tornado):\n1. Operating margin: quarterly op margin <4.0% \u2192 thesis broken.\n2. Capex: FY27 capex >$280M without revenue acceleration \u2192 negative signal.\n3. Chile: sales/club <$80M after 2 years \u2192 strategic failure. \n4. COP/USD >3,600 average for FY27 \u2192 downgrade revenue path 300bp.\nQuarterly: compare reported vs constant-currency comps; membership renewal rate; club count vs plan (6 by mid-2027); insider 13D/Form 4 cluster sells.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 9. RED TEAM \u2014 ADVERSARIAL ANALYSIS\n\nStep 9-1. Short Thesis:\n1. Price $174.29 sits ABOVE the highest analyst target ($165) and 71% above my base IV; trailing P/E 34.6x for a 2.8% net-margin retailer is history-rich. [Actual/Estimate]\n2. FCF yield (TTM, after SBC) \u2248 1.0% \u2014 the market is paying growth prices for a company whose reinvestment cycle consumes all cash. [Arithmetic]\n3. COP at a 7-year high and BBVA modeling 3,600+ by 2027 \u2192 15%+ translation headwind to 20% of revenue. [Estimate]\n4. Q3 FY26: revenue beat but adjusted EPS miss ($1.28 vs $1.32) \u2014 costs are creeping. [Estimate]\n5. Chile is not a greenfield monopoly \u2014 Walmart (Lider) and Cencosud operate there; first-mover advantage is weaker than in Central America. [Estimate]\n6. At 8.7% WACC, the market needs ~2.7x operating income by FY31 just to justify flat returns. [Arithmetic/Assumption]\n7. 98.94% institutional ownership means no marginal buyer; 9.76% short interest suggests informed skeptics. [Aggregator]\n\nStep 9-2. Logic Combat (Long vs Short):\nLong: membership economics (3), comp momentum (3), no-Costco moat (2), balance sheet (3), insider ownership (2), digital optionality (1) = 14/18 (78%).\nShort: valuation vs IV/targets (5), FCF yield (5), FX path (4), EPS miss/cost creep (3), Chile competition (3), reverse-DCF impossibility (5), positioning (3) = 28/32 (88%). Gap > 2 \u2192 conviction in SELL maintained.\n\nStep 9-3. Adversarial sources: no Hindenburg-style report [Unconfirmed]; MarketBeat article \"Why PriceSmart's discount may not last much longer\" [Estimate, title only]; tax: \"emerging growth company\" status [Estimate].\n\nStep 9-4. Killed Arguments:\n- \"Costco multiple applies\" \u2014 killed because Costco's membership income is ~60% of gross profit vs PSMT's 36% of operating income, and Costco has global scale.\n- \"Chile = next Costa Rica\" \u2014 killed: Chile has existing large-format grocery competitors; PSMT is entering with 1-2 clubs, not a network.\n- \"PEG 1.83 is fair\" \u2014 the vendor's PEG uses trailing EPS growth (18.9%); forward consensus growth is 15.4%, so forward PEG \u2248 2.25 [Arithmetic].\nRevival conditions: if Costco announces no LatAm entry AND Chile FY28 sales/club > $100M AND membership reaches 2% of merchandise sales, the bull case re-opens the file.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 10. AUDIT RESULTS\n\nA. Methodology: Engine 1 selected with sources; multi-method cross-check \u2014 PASS.\nB. Valuation: explicit 8-yr forecast; WACC derived; SBC deducted; leases included \u2014 PASS.\nC. Bias: overconfidence audit \u2014 base IV lowered from first-pass $107 to $102 when Q3 EPS miss and BBVA FX forecast were included \u2014 PASS (adjustment demonstrated).\nD. Consistency: no mixing of core and optionality (Chile modeled as capex drag, not as option premium); no time-point mixing (T0 price vs FY25 filings vs TTM filings separated) \u2014 PASS.\nE. Narrative: TAM narrative not used; numeric KPIs used \u2014 PASS.\nF. Red team: Sections 9/4.5/3.5 executed \u2014 PASS.\nG. Data integrity: [Unconfirmed] items declared (DXY, formal capex guidance, analyst Q&A, costco entry). Vendor total debt reconciled exactly to the sum of the four debt cells \u2014 PASS.\nH. Rule check: no DCF-only, no multiple-only, no TAM-only, no \"Buy\" without sizing, no \"large long-term potential\" language, base rates used, pre-mortem done \u2014 ALL PASS.\nI. Layer 5 vs 6.5 gap: 0% vs 0% \u2014 no conflict.\n\nPre-Mortem (mandatory): \"PriceSmart fails by 2031.\" Why? (1) Chile deployed $400M+ at sub-scale returns; (2) COP collapsed 25% in a LatAm EM crisis; (3) Costco entered Panama/Colombia via JV and undercut membership fees. Early warnings: Chile sales/club < $80M; COP spot > 3,800; Costco LatAm land purchases \u