ith constant-currency comps \u2265 +6%; (c) confirmation that Chile's first club is tracking to system-average productivity. Re-engage only with a recorded MoS \u2265 30%.\n\nKEY NUMBERS TO REMEMBER: Price $174.29 vs IV $102 (MoS \u221271%); trailing P/E 34.6x vs 12-yr median op margin 4.5%; required terminal FCF $408M vs my $267M; analyst high target $165; COP 3,100 spot and 3,628 BBVA 2026 average \u2014 the gap between price and fundamentals is the entire story.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 END OF ANALYSIS \u2014 RS2 v2.0 | Data as of 2026-08-24 | All unlabeled figures carry the tags shown at first use\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n```"}, {"sample": 3, "iv": 110.0, "plausible": true, "reasons": [], "flags": [], "truncated": false, "secs": 437, "report": "```markdown\n# RS2 v2.0 INTEGRATED INVESTMENT ANALYSIS \u2014 PSMT (PriceSmart, Inc.)\n**T0 Price:** $174.29 | **T0 Market Cap:** $5.379B | **T0 Date:** 2026-08-24\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 0 \u2014 ANALYSIS OVERVIEW\n\n- **Enterprise Archetype:** B \u2014 Quality Compounder [Step 0-1]\n  - 3+ reasons: (1) 12-year operating margin median 4.5% with low volatility; (2) ROIC estimated 14.4% > WACC; (3) membership-based recurring income (renewal 90.5%, Platinum 21.3% of members) and consistent 10%+ revenue growth; (4) no meaningful debt (net debt ~$0.015B).\n- **Valuation Engine:** Engine 1 \u2014 Traditional DCF + Multiples.\n- **Archetype Transition:** No transition. Stable, no trigger conditions met.\n- **Reason for Engine:** Quality Compounders are best valued on FCFF discounted at a risk-adjusted WACC, cross-checked with P/E multiples. A pure multiple conclusion would ignore the reinvestment required for Chile/club expansion; a TAM narrative would ignore the modest operating margin.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 1 \u2014 MACRO CONTEXT\n\n**Macro Variables [Actual unless noted]:**\n- 10Y US Treasury: **4.71%** \u2014 upward trend, highest since early 2025 (TradingEconomics, 2026-08-21).\n- Fed funds target range: **3.50%\u20133.75%**, held for fifth consecutive meeting; 3 dissenters favored a hike (FOMC, 2026-07-29).\n- CPI: **3.4%** y/y in July 2026, down from 3.5% in June (BLS).\n- DXY: **98.9**, weak dollar trend (TradingEconomics, 2026-08-25).\n- Growth/inflation backdrop: Elevated rates, above-target inflation, hawkish Fed split. Global liquidity neutral-to-tightening.\n\n**Regime Probability Distribution (must sum to 100%):**\n| Regime | Probability | Rationale |\n|---|---:|---|\n| Stagflation | 35% | CPI 3.4%, hawkish Fed, 10Y 4.71%, mixed growth |\n| Reflation | 30% | Weak dollar, consumer demand resilient, goods inflation |\n| Goldilocks | 20% | Low but positive growth, full employment |\n| Recession | 15% | Tightening bias if inflation persists |\n\n**Company Macro Sensitivity (-3 to +3):**\n- Interest rates: **-1** \u2014 modest impact on consumer credit; low leverage.\n- Dollar: **-2** \u2014 weak USD benefits reported USD revenue from LatAm.\n- Liquidity: **+1** \u2014 low debt, cash + ST investments $0.314B.\n- Economic cycle: **+1** \u2014 discount/warehouse model benefits from trade-down.\n- Commodities: **-2** \u2014 food price inflation pressures gross margin, partially passed through.\n\n**Macro Alignment:** **Neutral-to-Headwind.** Weighted sensitivity slightly negative from FX volatility and food inflation. Conviction adjust: -0.5 steps.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 1.5 \u2014 BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\n**Base Rates [Assumption / reference]:**\n- Quality Compounders with positive FCF and no leverage: 5-year survival probability **~90%**.\n- Successful entry into a new LatAm country with established retail infrastructure (Chile): **~55\u201365%**.\n- Warehouse-club comparable sales maintenance above mid-single-digit for 10 years: **~30%**.\n\n**Deviation vs Base Rate:**\n- Current 10.7% comparable sales (6.9% constant currency) is far above the 3\u20134% base rate for mature club operators. Justified by: (1) Colombia store maturation (35.7% comp), (2) Platinum membership penetration lifting revenue per member, (3) 6 net-new club pipeline.\n- Because success probability embedded in the model is ~1.5x the base rate for Chile, additional evidence was required. The Q3 FY26 transcript shows a signed Las Condes lease, ~20 Chile employees, and a defined $100M capex envelope. This is sufficient for a base-case assumption, not a bull-case assumption.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 2 \u2014 BUSINESS QUALITY\n\n**Business in 3 sentences:** PriceSmart operates membership warehouse clubs across Central America, the Caribbean, Colombia, and now Chile. Members pay annual fees; the company monetizes membership income, private label, and volume purchasing in markets underserved by large US peers. It currently operates ~57 clubs and plans six net new clubs, reaching 63 clubs.\n\n**Understandability:** **High.**\n\n**Moat Assessment (0\u201321):**\n| Moat type | Score |\n|---|---:|\n| Intangible assets (brand) | 2 |\n| Switching costs (membership) | 3 |\n| Network effects | 1 |\n| Cost advantage | 3 |\n| Economies of scale | 2 |\n| Regulatory barriers | 1 |\n| Data moat (member data) | 2 |\n| **Total** | **14 \u2014 Wide Moat** |\n\n**Moat Direction:** **Widening** \u2014 record renewal 90.5%, Platinum penetration up to 21.3% from 16.1%, digital sa